{"id": 1929816, "name": "SEIGLER, v. SOUTHERN RY.", "name_abbreviation": "Seigler v. Southern Ry.", "decision_date": "1910-03-22", "docket_number": "7517", "first_page": "345", "last_page": "346", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "85 S.C. 345"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "83 S. C., 530", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [1926344], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/83/0530-01"]}, {"cite": "59 S. C., 81", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4415235], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/59/0081-01"]}, {"cite": "17 S. C., 481", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [363255], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/17/0481-01"]}, {"cite": "6 S. C., 126", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4371738], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/6/0126-01"]}, {"cite": "65 S. C., 116", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "21 S. C., 27", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [2343287], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/21/0027-01"]}, {"cite": "17 S. C., 481", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [363255], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/17/0481-01"]}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 209, "char_count": 2771, "ocr_confidence": 0.588, "pagerank": {"raw": 1.3275324736781876e-07, "percentile": 0.6282429979508458}, "sha256": "3354e4e2786897de2a154e6b08ae678c050f178535a55e1152b142043345184f", "simhash": "1:7195f294e42cde17", "word_count": 501}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T19:00:45.680843+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["SEIGLER, v. SOUTHERN RY."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Hydrick.\nDuring the progress of the trial in the Circuit Court, plaintiff\u2019s attorney announced that he had found that the order appointing the guardian ad litem for plaintiff, who was not of age when the action was commenced, had, by some oversight, not been signed by the clerk, and asked leave to have the clerk sign it nunc pro tunc; and, failing in that, he asked leave to amend his complaint, by striking out the allegation of plaintiff\u2019s minority and the appointment of a guardian ad litem, and to continue the action in the name of the plaintiff, as he was then of age.\nThe presiding Judge refused both motions, in a formal order, in which he says that he would have granted one or the other, if he had had the power to do so*.\n1 Under the liberal provisions of sections 194 and 195 of the Code, as to the allowance of amendments, there can be no doubt that the Court had the power to grant either motion, and should have done so. Boyce v. Lake, 17 S. C., 481. As the plaintiff had become of age since the commencement of the action, the proper method of procedure was to strike from the complaint the allegation as to his nonage and the appointment of a guardian ad litem, and continue the action in his own name.\nMoreover, the plaintiff\u2019s capacity to sue was not in issue. As to the allegation of the appointment of a guardian ad litem, the answer was \u201cthat the defendant has no knowledge or information sufficient to form a belief.\u201d In Steamship Co. v. Rodgers, 21 S. C., 27, it was held, \u201cthis is only one of the modes of making a general denial,\u201d and such a denial does not put in issue the plaintiff\u2019s capacity to sue. Blackwell v. Mortgage Co., 65 S. C., 116, 43 & E., 395, and the cases cited.\nReversed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Hydrick."}], "attorneys": ["Mr. Wm. N. Gray don, for appellant,", "Messrs. Cothran, Dean & Cothran, contra."], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "7517\nSEIGLER, v. SOUTHERN RY.\n1. Amending Pleadings \u2014 Guardian ad Litem \u2014 Minor.\u2014A Circuit Judge during trial may grant a motion to have an order appointing a guardian ad litem signed hy the clerk then nunc pro tunc or he may grant a motion to strike from the complaint all allegations referring to the minority of plaintiff and appointment of guardian if minor has arrived at his majority, which is the better practice.\n9. Capacity to Sue \u2014 Minor.\u2014Answering the allegation of appointment of guardian ad litem by saying defendant has no knowledge or information sufficient to form a belief, does not put in issue plaintiff\u2019s capacity to sue.\nBefore Shipp, J., Greenwood, Fall Term, 1909.\nReversed.\nAction by Thomas Seigler by guardian ad litem against Southern Ry. Co. From order refusing motion to amend, plaintiff appeals.\nMr. Wm. N. Gray don, for appellant,\ncites: 6 S. C., 126; 17 S. C., 481; 59 S. C., 81; 83 S. C., 530.\nMessrs. Cothran, Dean & Cothran, contra.\nOral argument.\nMarch 22, 1910."}, "file_name": "0345-01", "first_page_order": 361, "last_page_order": 362}